Chapter: PoseSpec
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Safari chapter Pose
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 Pose, whose whole risk is a copy that transposes two fields.

 The chapter is a record and two functions, and it exists to cut an import cycle
 rather than to compute anything. That makes it look like nothing worth grading.
 It is the opposite: `with-tilt` rebuilds a NINE-FIELD record by hand, naming
 every field on both sides, and a copy written that way fails by swapping two
 names rather than by getting a number wrong.

 SUCH A SWAP IS NEARLY INVISIBLE TO THE GAME'S OWN CHECKS. Seven of the nine
 fields are 0.0 in `initial-rider-state`, and `yaw = s.gaze-yaw` is the identity
 on any state where both are zero. A transposition between two zero fields
 survives every frame the rider spends going straight, which is most of them,
 and then appears somewhere else entirely on the first turn.

 SO THE PROBE STATE BELOW HAS NINE PAIRWISE-DISTINCT VALUES, none of them zero,
 and the replacement tilt is distinct from all nine. That is the whole design of
 this file: under it every one of the 72 wrong field pairings changes an answer,
 where under the initial state almost none of them do. The values are otherwise
 arbitrary -- they are dyadic rationals so that every one is exact in binary and
 the grading can be at 0.0.

 WHERE THE EXPECTED VALUES COME FROM: the definition of a copy. `with-tilt s t`
 must return s with tilt replaced by t and NOTHING else touched, so the want is
 the probe's own field values with one substitution, written out. Nothing was
 captured; there is nothing here an implementation could have told us.

 We say:

Section: The Probe State

 Nine distinct non-zero values, so that no two fields can be confused without
 the confusion showing.

  probe : RiderState
  probe = RiderState { segment = 7, along = 11.0, across = 2.5, yaw = 0.75, v = 3.25, tilt = 0.125, heading = 4.5, gaze-yaw = 0.375, focus = 6.75 }

  new-tilt : Real
  new-tilt = 9.5

Section: Reading The Record Back

 The eight Reals in declaration order, and the segment separately because it is
 an Integer. Both are read, because a copy can carry the Reals and drop the one
 field that is not one.

  reals : RiderState -> List Real
  reals (s) = [s.along, s.across, s.yaw, s.v, s.tilt, s.heading, s.gaze-yaw, s.focus]

  seg : RiderState -> List Integer
  seg (s) = [s.segment]

Section: The Initial State

 A pin rather than a derivation, and worth having for one field: `v` starts at
 `v-base` and not at rest, which is the only non-zero value in the record and
 the one a careless edit would zero.

  init-want : List Real
  init-want = [0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.3, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]

  init-seg-want : List Integer
  init-seg-want = [0]

Section: The Copy

 The probe's own values with tilt, and only tilt, replaced.

  tilt-want : List Real
  tilt-want = [11.0, 2.5, 0.75, 3.25, 9.5, 4.5, 0.375, 6.75]

  tilt-seg-want : List Integer
  tilt-seg-want = [7]

 APPLYING IT TWICE MUST EQUAL APPLYING IT ONCE, which is a different assertion
 from the one above rather than a restatement of it. The line above compares a
 copy against a written-out answer; this one compares two copies against each
 other and so catches a field that is carried WRONGLY BUT CONSISTENTLY -- read
 from the wrong source on both passes, agreeing with the want by coincidence
 because the probe was built to make the two agree. It costs one more line.

  twice-want : List Real
  twice-want = [11.0, 2.5, 0.75, 3.25, 9.5, 4.5, 0.375, 6.75]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "p-init  " (reals initial-rider-state) init-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "p-initsg" (seg initial-rider-state) init-seg-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "p-tilt  " (reals (with-tilt probe new-tilt)) tilt-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "p-tiltsg" (seg (with-tilt probe new-tilt)) tilt-seg-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "p-twice " (reals (with-tilt (with-tilt probe 1.5) new-tilt)) twice-want 0.0)
  end
